{"id":"other-pay-by-department","title":"Pay beyond salary and overtime (“other pay”), by department","tier":"REDFLAG","question":"How much is paid outside regular salary and overtime, which departments pay most of it, and how has it moved?","finding":"Pay recorded as “other” came to $282.5 million in fiscal 2026, 5.8% of regular salary; it was $135.5 million (4.5%) in fiscal 2017 and $152.1 million (6.2%) in fiscal 2013. Police, Public Health and Fire Department paid 61% of it. In Police it rose from $19.1 million (5.9% of salary) in fiscal 2017 to $63.2 million (15.1%) in fiscal 2026, about $20,940 for each person paid. The payroll file does not say what the payments were; the budget analyst wrote in 2024 that the police agreement in force from July 2023 brought “wage increases, retention pay, step adjustments, and signing bonus”.","how":"City payroll file, fiscal 2013 to 2026: the “other salaries” column, which the file’s own dictionary describes as “Various irregular payments made to City employee including premium pay, incentive pay, or other one-time payments”. Added up by department for everyone on the file except the Superior Court; the share is of the same people’s regular salary; “per person” divides by the number of names paid in the department that year. Fiscal 2017 is the first year with names, after the move to a new data system that the file’s dictionary mentions, which is why it is shown beside fiscal 2013. In this file a full-time year is 2,080 paid hours in every fiscal year from 2017 to 2025 and 2,160 in fiscal 2026 (27 fortnights of 80 hours rather than 26), so every yearly amount for fiscal 2026 is about 3.8% larger than a like-for-like year.","limits":" The file’s dictionary names premium pay, incentive pay and one-time payments, and the City’s accounts say staff are paid for unused vacation, sick leave and time off in lieu; none of these is separated in the public file, so this table cannot say how much is which. A department with round-the-clock shifts, such as police, fire and the hospitals, pays more premiums for ordinary reasons. The fall from $159.2 million in fiscal 2016 to $135.5 million in fiscal 2017 coincides with the move to a new data system that the file’s dictionary mentions, and may be a change in what was recorded under this heading rather than in what was paid. The pay codes behind the column, which the Controller holds, would settle the composition.","columns":[{"k":"department","t":"Department","type":"text"},{"k":"people","t":"People paid, fiscal 2026","type":"int"},{"k":"other","t":"Other pay, fiscal 2026","type":"usd"},{"k":"share","t":"As a share of regular salary","type":"pct"},{"k":"perPerson","t":"Per person paid","type":"usd"},{"k":"otherThen","t":"Other pay, fiscal 2017","type":"usd"},{"k":"shareThen","t":"Share of salary, fiscal 2017","type":"pct"},{"k":"shareFirst","t":"Share of salary, fiscal 2013","type":"pct"},{"k":"added","t":"Added since fiscal 2017","type":"usd"}],"rows":[{"department":"Police","people":3020,"other":63239806,"share":0.1506,"perPerson":20940,"otherThen":19105419,"shareThen":0.0589,"shareFirst":0.1224,"added":44134387},{"department":"Public Health","people":8913,"other":59346224,"share":0.0489,"perPerson":6658,"otherThen":34229181,"shareThen":0.0505,"shareFirst":0.0691,"added":25117043},{"department":"Fire Department","people":2067,"other":49418157,"share":0.1668,"perPerson":23908,"otherThen":25884548,"shareThen":0.1307,"shareFirst":0.1326,"added":23533610},{"department":"Municipal Transportation Agency","people":6213,"other":40155683,"share":0.0642,"perPerson":6463,"otherThen":18672806,"shareThen":0.0446,"shareFirst":0.0573,"added":21482877},{"department":"Sheriff","people":1067,"other":14863597,"share":0.1096,"perPerson":13930,"otherThen":10244338,"shareThen":0.1065,"shareFirst":0.114,"added":4619259},{"department":"Public Utilities Commission","people":2735,"other":10908827,"share":0.0294,"perPerson":3989,"otherThen":6007634,"shareThen":0.0277,"shareF